{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>If there be your prophet.</b> If Moses were a prophet at that level you supposed when you said: הֲלֹא גַם בָּנוּ דִבֵּר (Numbers 12:2)." "<b>In a vision I make Myself known to him — by the name YHWH.</b> I would not make Myself known to him and reveal Myself upon him by this Name except in a vision of feminine aspect — not while awake, as was the case with Isaiah when he said: וָאֶרְאֶה אֶת ה׳ יוֹשֵׁב עַל כִּסֵּא רָם וְנִשָּׂא (Isaiah 6:1). And likewise Micah: וְרָאִיתִי אֶת ה׳ יוֹשֵׁב עַל כִּסְאוֹ (I Kings 22:19) — and all this in a prophetic vision, without doubt, not while awake. And also Balaam: even though the speech came to him while awake, he did not have it with the distinctive Name, as he supposed when he said: אוּלַי יִקָּרֶה ה׳ לִקְרָאתִי (Numbers 23:3) — for this indeed did not come to pass for him, but rather it was said of him: וַיִּקָּר אֱלֹהִים אֶל בִּלְעָם (Numbers 23:4)."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "The lemma as printed combines the end of v. 6b (אֵלָיו אֶתְוַדָּע) with ה׳ from the phrase ה׳ בַּמַּרְאָה; Sforno's point is that revelation by the Name YHWH occurs only in vision, not while awake. 'Feminine aspect' renders לְשׁוֹן נְקֵבָה, i.e., the grammatically feminine noun מַרְאָה as opposed to the masculine חָזוֹן used for higher prophecy."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:39:33.266710046Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}